BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric engineer compares two seersucker fabric production methods: ''woven seersucker'' (achieved by holding SOME warp ends at DIFFERENT tension during weaving — slack-tension ends pucker while normal-tension ends stay flat, creating the characteristic crinkled stripe pattern purely from a mechanical/structural weaving technique) versus ''chemical seersucker'' (achieved by printing a caustic-soda-resistant/shrinking agent in a striped pattern onto a FLAT fabric AFTER weaving, causing differential shrinkage in the printed vs. unprinted zones). What is the KEY functional/durability difference between these two approaches?', '["Both methods produce mathematically identical fabric with zero functional or durability difference, making the distinction between weaving-based and chemical-based seersucker purely a matter of production method preference with no product-quality consequence","Woven seersucker''s puckered texture is a PERMANENT STRUCTURAL feature (built directly into the differential warp tension during weaving itself, similar in principle to the earlier double-cloth/structural-engineering discussion) that persists through the fabric''s entire life regardless of washing/wear, while chemical seersucker''s texture depends on a topically-applied chemical treatment that can potentially FADE or reduce in intensity over repeated washing/wear (similar to the earlier finish-durability discussion), since it''s a surface/chemical effect rather than a built-in structural one","Chemical seersucker always produces a MORE permanent, wash-durable texture than woven seersucker, the reverse of the actual relationship between these two production approaches","Seersucker texture, regardless of production method, is always immediately and completely lost after the very first wash for both methods equally, making any comparison between their relative durability technically irrelevant"]'::jsonb, 'Woven seersucker''s puckered texture is a PERMANENT STRUCTURAL feature (built directly into the differential warp tension during weaving itself, similar in principle to the earlier double-cloth/structural-engineering discussion) that persists through the fabric''s entire life regardless of washing/wear, while chemical seersucker''s texture depends on a topically-applied chemical treatment that can potentially FADE or reduce in intensity over repeated washing/wear (similar to the earlier finish-durability discussion), since it''s a surface/chemical effect rather than a built-in structural one', 'This applies the general ''structural vs. surface-treatment durability'' principle (established across several earlier discussions — softener durability, FR-treatment durability, dye fixation durability) to seersucker''s distinctive texture specifically — woven seersucker''s puckering comes from a genuine STRUCTURAL mechanism (differential warp tension built directly into the weave itself during production, permanently locked into the fiber/fabric geometry), while chemical seersucker achieves a visually similar effect through a topically-applied chemical/print treatment that, like other surface treatments discussed earlier, can potentially lose effectiveness over repeated washing/wear — a genuine durability distinction between a structural and a chemical-treatment approach to achieving the same visual/textural effect.', 3, '{"woven-vs-chemical-seersucker"}'::text[]),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric mill''s technologist explains that ''crepe'' fabric''s characteristic crinkled, pebbly surface texture can be achieved through THREE different mechanisms: high-twist crepe yarn (using yarn with unusually high, often alternating S/Z twist that causes puckering when relaxed, similar to the earlier yarn-twist-liveliness discussion), a crepe WEAVE structure (an irregular interlacing pattern creating surface texture from structure alone, using normal-twist yarn), or a chemical/embossing crepe finish. If a buyer specifically requires the crepe texture to survive HIGH-temperature commercial laundering (e.g., for a hospitality/hotel textile application) without flattening, which mechanism is generally the MOST durable choice, and why?', '["All three crepe mechanisms are equally durable under any laundering condition, making mechanism choice irrelevant to this specific durability requirement","High-twist crepe YARN is generally the most durable mechanism for this application, since the texture originates from PERMANENT molecular-level yarn twist (similar to how the earlier yarn-twist-liveliness discussion established that twist-related structural effects are inherent to the yarn itself, not a surface treatment), making it inherently more resistant to high-temperature laundering than either a chemical/embossing finish (a surface treatment subject to the same durability limitations as other surface finishes discussed earlier) or potentially even structural crepe weave (which, while also durable, relies on interlacing geometry that could be more susceptible to being pressed flat under high heat/pressure commercial laundering than the yarn''s own inherent twist-driven texture)","Chemical/embossing crepe finish is always the most durable choice for high-temperature commercial laundering, the reverse of the actual relationship given the earlier-established surface-treatment durability limitations","Crepe texture mechanism has no relationship to laundering durability, making this entire technical comparison irrelevant to the buyer''s stated hospitality/hotel textile requirement"]'::jsonb, 'High-twist crepe YARN is generally the most durable mechanism for this application, since the texture originates from PERMANENT molecular-level yarn twist (similar to how the earlier yarn-twist-liveliness discussion established that twist-related structural effects are inherent to the yarn itself, not a surface treatment), making it inherently more resistant to high-temperature laundering than either a chemical/embossing finish (a surface treatment subject to the same durability limitations as other surface finishes discussed earlier) or potentially even structural crepe weave (which, while also durable, relies on interlacing geometry that could be more susceptible to being pressed flat under high heat/pressure commercial laundering than the yarn''s own inherent twist-driven texture)', 'This applies the same structural-vs-surface-treatment durability hierarchy established across multiple earlier discussions to a specific buyer requirement — high-twist crepe yarn''s texture originates from genuine, permanent residual twist torque built into the yarn itself (the SAME underlying physical property established in the earlier yarn-twist-liveliness/spirality discussions, here deliberately EXPLOITED for a desired texture rather than being an unwanted defect), making it inherently resistant to being ''undone'' by heat/laundering in a way a purely chemical/embossed surface treatment (subject to the same wash-durability limitations discussed for softeners and other surface finishes) is not — for a demanding high-temperature commercial laundering application, this yarn-level, structurally-inherent mechanism generally offers the most robust, lasting texture durability.', 3, '{"crepe-mechanism-durability"}'::text[]),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric technologist explains ''dobby'' weave capability limits: a specific dobby head model supports a MAXIMUM of 24 heald shafts. If a designer wants an 8-end SATIN weave repeat CROSSED with a separate 6-end pattern element (requiring the combined design''s total repeat to use shafts equal to the LEAST COMMON MULTIPLE of the two pattern elements'' shaft requirements, not simply their sum), can this specific 24-shaft dobby head support this combined design?', '["LCM(8,6) = 24 — this exactly matches the dobby head''s maximum capacity of 24 shafts, so the design is right at the edge of feasibility, technically supportable but with zero spare shaft capacity for any further design refinement","LCM(8,6) = 48, incorrectly doubling instead of correctly finding the least common multiple, which would mean this design EXCEEDS the 24-shaft capacity and is NOT supportable on this dobby head","The combined shaft requirement is simply 8+6=14, well within the 24-shaft capacity with significant spare capacity remaining","Shaft requirements for combined weave elements are never calculated using least common multiple; they are always calculated as the larger of the two individual numbers alone (8), making this an even simpler calculation than either LCM or sum"]'::jsonb, 'LCM(8,6) = 24 — this exactly matches the dobby head''s maximum capacity of 24 shafts, so the design is right at the edge of feasibility, technically supportable but with zero spare shaft capacity for any further design refinement', 'The Least Common Multiple of 8 and 6: 8=2³, 6=2×3, LCM=2³×3=24 — this exactly matches the dobby head''s 24-shaft maximum, meaning the combined design is right at the boundary of feasibility on this specific machine, with zero spare shaft capacity remaining for any further design adjustment or additional pattern elements — a designer/technologist should recognize this as a tight, maximum-capacity fit requiring careful design finalization before committing to production, rather than assuming ample remaining capacity exists, and should understand that shaft requirements for combined pattern elements follow LCM logic (reflecting when both pattern repeats simultaneously complete a full cycle), not simple addition or simply taking the larger individual number.', 3, '{"dobby-shaft-capacity-LCM"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
